import javax.swing.JFrame;
import javax.swing.JTextField;

public class SalaryGUI {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int hourlyWage = 20;

        // Text field that shows the output
        JTextField outputField = new JTextField();
        outputField.setText("An hourly wage of " + hourlyWage + "/hr"
                + " yields $" + (hourlyWage * 40 * 50) + "/yr.");
        outputField.setEditable(false);

        // Window
        JFrame topFrame = new JFrame("Salary");
        topFrame.add(outputField);
        topFrame.pack();
        topFrame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        topFrame.setVisible(true);
    }
}

Check Your Understanding
1) JFrame is the application window. It holds the other components.
2) It is a text box that displays the program's output inside the window.
3) The hourly rate and the yearly total shown in the window both change, because the total is calculated from hourlyWage.
4) It makes the field read-only, so the user can see the result but cannot type over it.
5) It resizes the window to fit its components.
6) It makes the program end when the window is closed. Without it, the window disappears but the program can keep running in the background.
7) The program runs, but the window never appears on screen.
8) The yearly total increases by two weeks of pay. At $20/hr that is 2 × 40 × $20 = $1,600, so $40,000 becomes $41,600.
